WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.590
- [Narrator] NASA has been gearing up

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:03.300
to return astronauts to the moon

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.060
for the first time in
more than half a century.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.520
- [Astronaut] Hey, they've
got the flag up now,

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:08.538
and you can see the

00:00:08.538 --> 00:00:09.581
stars and stripes.
(microphone feedback)

00:00:09.581 --> 00:00:10.650
- [Narrator] As soon as the end of August,

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:13.440
the Space Agency plans to
conduct the first launch

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.970
of the massive rocket system

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:17.910
for its moon exploration program, Artemis.

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.850
The first mission, Artemis
I, will send a spacecraft

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.100
on an uncrewed trip around the moon.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.530
- So Artimis I is a test run.

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:28.380
It's a test run for the rocket system,

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:29.550
for the the spacecraft

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:32.580
for the ground systems,
the ground infrastructure.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.020
- [Narrator] It will be a critical step

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:37.230
towards landing people
on the moon by 2025.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:38.940
But over the last few years,

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.580
NASA and its partners
have experienced a range

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.820
of technical, legal,
and cost-related delays.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.100
- There's a lot of elements that you have

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:47.933
to get right,

00:00:47.933 --> 00:00:49.410
that NASA and its partners have

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:50.243
to get right

00:00:50.243 --> 00:00:53.760
to sort of make all that
technical choreography work.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.170
- [Narrator] So how does NASA plan

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:56.739
to achieve this historic mission

00:00:56.739 --> 00:00:59.340
and how could Artemis I pave the way

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:01.110
for future space exploration

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:02.793
beyond the lunar surface?

00:01:04.683 --> 00:01:05.516
(camera shuttering)

00:01:05.516 --> 00:01:07.140
There are several key
space vehicles involved

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.780
in NASA's effort to get
humans back on the moon.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.137
- So there's the SLS,

00:01:12.137 --> 00:01:13.890
that's the kind of rocket system

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.480
that starts the journey,

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:18.600
and on top of that is
Orion which separates

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:23.010
from the rocket and then begins
its journey to lunar orbit.

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:23.843
- [Narrator] There will also be

00:01:23.843 --> 00:01:25.680
a separate lunar Lander designed

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.900
to take astronauts from lunar orbit down

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.280
to the moon surface.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.800
That lander designed by SpaceX is expected

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.740
to be used on a future Artemis
launch currently planned

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.018
for 2025.

00:01:36.018 --> 00:01:39.540
The first mission, Artemis
I, will send the crew capsule

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:42.510
into orbit around the
moon for about six weeks,

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.220
allowing NASA to test a series

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.800
of critical systems like life support

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.110
before it puts people on board.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.300
Artemis II will follow a similar path,

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.310
but with astronauts on board.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:57.090
And Artemis III will carry
people to the moon surface.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.190
NASA says this mission will be historic

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:01.140
for other reasons as well.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:02.910
- It's opening the door
(camera shuttering)

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:05.439
for the first woman and
the first person of color

00:02:05.439 --> 00:02:07.530
to set foot on the moon.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:08.640
- [Narrator] Artemis I will serve

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.340
as a pathfinding mission
laying a foundation

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:12.930
for future trips to the moon.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.790
It will also be the first test flight

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:17.760
of NASA's new space
launch system, Megarocket,

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.770
and the Orion crew capsule.

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:21.840
Another key objective will be testing the

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.370
spacecraft's heat shield.
(bell dinging)

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:25.260
It's meant to protect the Orion capsule

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:27.060
from the extreme temperatures

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:30.990
it will encounter entering
Earth's atmosphere at Mach 32,

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:35.070
which is around 24,500 miles per hour.

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:38.820
- The idea is to run all the
hardware, all the software

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.650
through its paces and show

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:44.040
that the rockets can
fire this spacecraft out

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.990
of the gravitational pull of earth,

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:49.200
and the spacecraft can separate and travel

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.980
to the moon and get back
to this planet safely.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.810
- [Narrator] NASA will also be monitoring

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:56.640
Orion's navigation systems

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.320
and its resilience when traveling

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.180
through high radiation areas.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.820
Three mannequins on board
will be fitted with sensors

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.970
to determine how astronauts
will fare on the flight.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.400
Another test will be
recovering the spacecraft

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.544
after it splashes down in the ocean.

00:03:10.544 --> 00:03:13.410
Meanwhile, the vehicles
that NASA plans to use

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:15.810
for Artemis I have faced delays.

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.460
The mission was originally scheduled

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:19.650
to take off in 2021.

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:23.220
- The contractors that are
building the different elements

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:27.630
of the SLS rocket system
and Orion have run

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:30.390
into technical challenges and have had

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:32.100
to work those out.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.410
They've faced supply chain problems.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:36.210
And some of these rockets,

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.810
NASA has not attempted
to have built and launch

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.370
in quite a long time.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:44.580
So part of the challenge was
like simply getting back up

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.620
to speed on creating again,

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.040
really big, really powerful rockets.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:51.240
- [Narrator] The first Artemis rocket

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.690
and the broader Artemis program
are expected to be costly.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:57.420
Last November, NASA's
inspector general forecasted

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:00.270
the agency would spend $93 billion

00:04:00.270 --> 00:04:03.060
over more than a dozen years on Artemis.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:05.340
With key costs for the
first launch expected

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.890
to be around 4.1 billion.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:10.080
NASA leaders have said
the agency is pushing

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:13.560
to reduce costs for Artemis
and streamline operations.

00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:14.550
- Some of the contractors

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:16.650
that are building the
vehicles have said part

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:19.560
of the cost question is because we've been

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:20.760
in this development mode

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.200
building these for the first time.

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.260
And when we build future
rockets and spacecraft

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:28.050
the cost should come
down because we're simply

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:31.770
in production mode and not
in this developmental stage

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:35.070
of creating the vehicles
for the first time.

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:36.840
- [Narrator] There are
more long-term ambitions

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:38.490
for NASA's Artemis programs.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.560
- Astronauts will live and work

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:44.400
in deep space and will develop
the science and technology

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.150
to send the first humans to Mars.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:49.650
- [Narrator] The Space Agency has plans

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:50.940
to build Gateway,

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.940
a multipurpose outpost orbiting the moon.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:56.250
It has also discussed
developing a base camp

00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:57.750
on the lunar surface.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:04:58.590
More broadly,

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:01.890
NASA says it's return to the
moon efforts will help prepare

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:04.410
for future exploration of Mars.

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:09.410
- Artemis now has a vision
of sending astronauts back

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:12.420
to the moon and creating sort

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:14.400
of a sustained infrastructure.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:17.880
You know, pursuing science,
pursuing exploration.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:20.490
And the idea is that NASA will build all

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:23.100
of that infrastructure
and know-how hardware

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:27.120
to sort of make that happen
and use that knowledge base

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.610
to pursue the next frontier,

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:32.760
which is some sort of trip to Mars.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:34.830
- [Narrator] As the Artemis
I launch date nears,

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:37.020
NASA is wrapping up several key tests

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:40.230
to ensure all the rocket
systems will be ready to go.

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:45.230
- That 2025 timeline for getting
people down to the surface

00:05:45.367 --> 00:05:49.350
of the moon and back is
coming up relatively quickly.

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:52.890
The pressure is on to sort
of get to that deadline,

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:55.563
and we'll see if they can make it happen.

00:05:56.588 --> 00:05:59.755
(gentle upbeat music)

